    "description": "Our design system needs an accessible tab set whose selection rules cannot be bypassed. WorkspaceTabs must own the active tab, coordinate focus and keyboard navigation, and associate each trigger with its panel using stable IDs. A TabTrigger declares its key, label, and disabled state; a TabPanel declares the content associated with that key. Consumers will place these children together in their desired order, and the same selection must be visible to triggers, panels, and automation that checks the active state. Individual children must not keep their own selected flag, because that would allow two panels to appear active. The API also needs to reject duplicate keys, omit disabled triggers from arrow-key navigation, and expose the active key to an onChange callback. We want consumers to compose the family declaratively without passing the current key and update handlers through every descendant.",
    "decision_statement": "Choose how WorkspaceTabs, TabTrigger, and TabPanel share and enforce one active-tab state while allowing consumers to declare the family in ordered JSX.",
    "notes": "Compound-components fits because WorkspaceTabs owns the active tab and family-wide invariants while triggers and panels register declaratively; custom-hook is the state-ownership alternative, headless-component and render-props support reuse."
  },
